            "detectedLanguage": "en",
            "pageCount": 1,
            "processed": true,
            "summary": "This document reports a case involving an 8-year-old girl who experienced adverse effects after taking Ferbolex syrup for a respiratory infection. The medication was administered at a dosage of 250mg twice daily for five days. Following the treatment, the girl developed jaundice and fatigue, which prompted further investigation. Liver function tests revealed significantly elevated ALT and AST enzyme levels—around six times higher than the normal upper limit. Upon discontinuing Ferbolex, the girl's liver enzyme levels began to normalize within three weeks.\n\nThis document is relevant to a patient safety review because it highlights a potential adverse drug reaction associated with Ferbolex. The occurrence of jaundice and elevated liver enzymes indicates a serious side effect that could have implications for other patients. The case underscores the importance of monitoring liver function in patients receiving this medication. It also raises questions about the drug’s safety profile, particularly in pediatric patients. Overall, this documentation is valuable for assessing drug safety and could inform future clinical practices and pharmacovigilance efforts.",
